<Project>

  <!--
    Heron AI - shared build configuration.

    ONE property controls which Revit generation is built:

        dotnet build -p:RevitVersion=2024

    The multi-targeting machinery is here from the first commit because it is
    cheap now and ruinous to retrofit (D-05, docs/16), and it is no longer a
    structure waiting to be used: tools/check-compile.py builds EVERY release
    in the table below, CI runs it on every push - the job is named "The C#
    compiles, 2020 to 2027" - and a real Revit has loaded the add-in on three
    of them (docs/NEEDS-CHECKING.md A12). This said "Step 1 proves ONE
    version; the structure already supports the rest" until 2026-09-21, which
    was true at Step 1 and has been out of date since (row 5b-68).

    Runtime per Revit generation - see docs/16-version-support-strategy.md:
        2020            net472
        2021-2024       net48
        2025-2026       net8.0-windows
        2027            net10.0-windows
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RevitVersion Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' == ''">2024</RevitVersion>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Configuration Condition="'$(Configuration)' == ''">Debug</Configuration>
    <Platform>x64</Platform>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>
    <AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>false</AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>true</GenerateAssemblyInfo>
    <Company>Ajmal PS</Company>
    <Product>Heron AI</Product>
    <Version>0.1.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ONE BUILD FOLDER PER REVIT RELEASE - added 2026-09-21, and it is a bug
    report as much as a setting.

    AppendTargetFrameworkToOutputPath is false above, so every release used to
    write to the SAME bin\x64\<Configuration>\. Building 2024 overwrote 2020;
    building 2027 overwrote 2024. Only the last one built survived, so only
    that one release could be installed.

    THE COST WAS PAID IN FULL. On 2026-09-21 the owner ticked all three
    releases on his PC in the installer window and pressed Install. Two were
    refused - correctly, by the runtime guard in deploy-addin.ps1 - because the
    only build on disk was 2027's. He tried four times. Nothing was broken; it
    simply could not work, and nothing said so until after the press.

    THE TESTS ALREADY KNEW. tests/test_bridge_roundtrip.py carries the comment
    "every target framework writes to the SAME bin/x64/Debug", and it,
    test_kernel.py, test_stack_guard.py and test_binding_note.py each pass
    -p:OutputPath=bin/x64/Debug-<tfm>/ by hand to dodge it. Four workarounds
    for a thing nobody had fixed. This is that fix, made once.

    SPLIT BY REVIT RELEASE, NOT BY TARGET FRAMEWORK. 2021 to 2024 all build
    net48, but they are NOT interchangeable: DefineConstants below gives each
    REVIT<year> and its _OR_GREATER siblings, so a 2021 build and a 2024 build
    of the same source differ. A per-framework split would let a 2021 build
    install into 2024 and never say a word - the exact class of defect A12
    recorded.

    IT MUST BE SET HERE, IN .props, AND NOT IN A .targets FILE. Measured with
    `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:` on 2026-09-21: setting OutputPath from
    Directory.Build.targets left OutDir AND ProjectDepsFilePath still pointing
    at the flat folder, because both are derived from OutputPath before a
    .targets file is imported. The build then wrote its assemblies to the new
    folder and its deps.json to the old one, and Revit 2027 would not load -
    "needs .NET runtime metadata and the build has none". A split output is
    worse than a shared one, because it looks like it worked.

    THE EXCEPTIONS ARE LISTED BY NAME, deliberately. A project that does not
    vary with the release keeps the flat path: the installer, its engine, its
    test host, and the API-surface reader are all net8.0 whatever Revit is
    being built for, and all are opened BY PATH - by a person double-clicking
    HeronInstaller.exe, or by a script. Burying them under a release number
    they do not have would be a worse lie than the collision this fixes.

    The list is here rather than a flag in each .csproj because a .csproj is
    read AFTER this file, so a flag set there cannot be seen in time - the same
    ordering that makes a .targets file too late. Adding a release-independent
    project means adding a line here, and check-structure.py has no opinion
    about it either way.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <HeronReleaseIndependent Condition="
        '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Heron.Installer'          OR
        '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Heron.Installer.App'      OR
        '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Heron.Installer.TestHost' OR
        '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'ApiSurface'">true</HeronReleaseIndependent>

    <OutputPath Condition="'$(HeronReleaseIndependent)' != 'true'">bin\$(Platform)\$(Configuration)\$(RevitVersion)\</OutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Map Revit version to its runtime.

    Autodesk moved the runtime at 2025 (.NET Framework to .NET 8) and again at
    2027 (.NET 8 to .NET 10). A version this table does not list is therefore an
    ERROR, never a guess: confirm the target in the Autodesk SDK for that
    release, then add a row here.

    This used to read '>= 2027', which silently claimed every future Revit is
    .NET 10 - and would have produced an add-in that does not load, with no
    warning at build time.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <HeronTfm Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' == '2020'">net472</HeronTfm>
    <HeronTfm Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2021' AND '$(RevitVersion)' &lt;= '2024'">net48</HeronTfm>
    <HeronTfm Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2025' AND '$(RevitVersion)' &lt;= '2026'">net8.0-windows</HeronTfm>
    <HeronTfm Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' == '2027'">net10.0-windows</HeronTfm>

    <!-- Keeps evaluation alive long enough to report the real problem below,
         instead of failing with an unexplained empty-TargetFramework error. -->
    <HeronVersionSupported Condition="'$(HeronTfm)' != ''">true</HeronVersionSupported>
    <HeronTfm Condition="'$(HeronTfm)' == ''">net48</HeronTfm>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Target Name="HeronCheckRevitVersion" BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild;Restore;CollectPackageReferences">
    <Error Condition="'$(HeronVersionSupported)' != 'true'"
           Text="Heron does not know which .NET runtime Revit $(RevitVersion) uses. Supported today: 2020 to 2027. Autodesk has moved the runtime twice (at 2025 and at 2027), so Heron will not guess - confirm the target for that release against the Autodesk SDK and add it to Directory.Build.props." />
  </Target>

  <!-- Compilation symbols so version-conditional code is possible. Per docs/16,
       such code belongs ONLY in adapters, never in core logic. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);REVIT$(RevitVersion)</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2024'">$(DefineConstants);REVIT2024_OR_GREATER</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2025'">$(DefineConstants);REVIT2025_OR_GREATER</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2026'">$(DefineConstants);REVIT2026_OR_GREATER</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(RevitVersion)' &gt;= '2027'">$(DefineConstants);REVIT2027_OR_GREATER</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
